[0:00–1:30, continued] Rio has its own version: in the Palácio do Catete, seat of the Presidency for 63 years, a Marianne in bronze and marble by the French sculptor Paul-Louis Émile Loiseau-Rousseau is on display today (museum record without date, so I say only “on display”). Here, in the old Palácio do Governo of Santa Catarina, refurbished in eclectic style between 1894 and 1898, the hall holds three cast busts: Marianne, Europa and América. The museum catalogue gives the foundry, Val d’Osne in Paris, nineteenth century, and attributes Europa and América to Mathurin Moreaux. Above them, as Clara Malossi and Alice Bononi showed this year at UDESC, the stucco panel brings Hermes, a probable Tyche on her winged wheel, and a child of abundance. One possible reading, which I present as interpretation: the Republic sits between Europe and America, under the protection of commerce and fortune. The transatlantic circulation this conference discusses is staged in the architecture of the palace itself. And the Marianne is a catalogue piece. The state of Santa Catarina acquired its figure of authority from a French foundry that cast in series, from a matrix. Keep that word, matrix: it will come back with the coin. What remains without public record: the author of the model and the date of acquisition.
2

THE QUESTION
When a female allegory represents the authority of the State, does she also authenticate it?
THESIS
The allegory represents power. When the law authenticates a coin, a seal or an act, it appeals to something else: matter, weight, value. The distance between the two is the object of this talk.
[around 1:00–1:30] So the question of this talk. When a woman in allegory represents the authority of the State, does she also authenticate it? My thesis in one sentence. The female allegory represents power. When the law authenticates, a coin, a seal, an act, it calls on something else: matter, weight, value. The distance between these two operations is my object today.
