[1:30–2:45] The circulation between France and Brazil can be followed in material terms, in three registers. Grammar. Cesare Ripa’s Iconologia travels as a manual of personifications. Debret arrives in Rio trained in Davidian neoclassicism and works within that tradition. Training. Décio Villares studies with Cabanel, exhibits at the Paris Salon of 1874, and declines a Parisian chair because he does not wish to become a French citizen. In 1889 he draws the globe of the republican flag and paints the Republic wearing a green Phrygian cap. [If 20 minutes: one more minute here. The Brazilian who would not become French dresses the Brazilian Republic in French grammar.] [If 10 minutes: cut Villares; keep Ripa and Debret only.] Body and matter. Marianne enters the palace as sculpture; French gold enters the bank reserve as law. We will reach that decree in a few minutes.
