[3:30–5:45] Before France and Brazil, a short genealogy, because the grammar that crosses the Atlantic was formed earlier. 1494, Sebastian Brant’s Ship of Fools. The blindfold is a satirical imposition: a fool covers Justice’s eyes. She is a character in a scene, someone something is done to, still relational. 1543, Bern, Hans Gieng’s Justice fountain. The blindfold has become an attribute of the civic emblem. Justice in office, settled, frontal. 1593, and the illustrated edition of 1603, Cesare Ripa’s Iconologia. The blindfold is naturalised as impartiality. It no longer needs a scene or an explanation. [If 10 minutes: one sentence per image.] (Dates follow the verification closed on 2 November; do not quote alternative dates without checking the record.)
