Debret, 1822: the Constitution on stage before the Constitution
Debret, Voyage pittoresque, vol. III (1839), pl. 49: “Pano de boca … por ocasião da Coroação do imperador D. Pedro I”.
Debret’s original watercolour, c. 1822, 21 × 35 cm. Collection: [to confirm].
1 Dec 1822. For the coronation of Pedro I, Debret paints the drop curtain of the court theatre. José Bonifácio approves the composition.
Centre. A seated, crowned woman with the sword and the tablets of the Constitution: the Imperial Government.
Anticipation. The tablets are on stage before any constitution exists; the Charter comes in March 1824.
1839. Plate 49 brings the scene to French readers.
Correction: Rosa, Rodrigues and Abraham (2020, p. 71-72) read the centre as the emperor; Debret’s own description (Silva, 2017, p. 32; Lima Junior, 2020, p. 136-139) names the female personification.
Ana Vanzin · Between Representation and Authentication
8
[6:30–9:00] Now the Atlantic. In December 1822, for the extraordinary performance at the court theatre on the occasion of the coronation of Pedro I, Jean-Baptiste Debret paints the drop curtain of the Real Teatro São João, replacing the Portuguese monarchical one. The plate’s own title says so: “por ocasião da Coroação”. The composition was submitted to José Bonifácio and approved by him: a ministerial commission under control, far from a private invention. At the centre, a seated, crowned female figure, with the sword and the tablets of the Constitution: the Imperial Government, constitutional royalty. A small correction to the literature. Rosa, Rodrigues and Abraham (2020) describe the central figure as the emperor himself. Debret’s own description, transcribed by Silva (2017, p. 32) and confirmed by Lima Junior (2020, p. 136-139), identifies the centre as the female personification. Then a useful inversion of time. In December 1822 the crowned woman already displays the tablets of the Constitution on the official stage, before the Constitution exists in law. The Constituent Assembly had yet to meet, and would be dissolved; the Charter comes in March 1824. The image anticipates a constitutional consensus that does not exist. Representation and legitimation operate in advance, and nothing is authenticated. In 1839 the same allegory circulates in France, as plate 49 of the third volume of the Voyage pittoresque, now for French readers. The Brazil that offers itself to be read in France is a Brazil whose sovereignty has a woman’s face. (The painted curtain itself did not survive. Two visual sources remain: Debret’s original watercolour, c. 1822, 21 × 35 cm, and the 1839 lithograph. Point at the small image: the watercolour stands closer to the 1822 commission; the plate is the version French readers saw.)
