Augustin Dupré’s winged genius writing the Constitution of 1793. Type struck 1871–1898, with strikes in 1889 and 1890.
PENAL CODE 1890, ART. 239: MOEDA FALSA
“… de identica materia, com a mesma forma, peso e valor intrinseco da verdadeira”
[my translation] “… of identical matter, with the same form, weight and intrinsic value as the genuine coin”
Authentication indexes matter, form, weight and intrinsic value. The effigy is absent from the offence.
Open question. The sole paragraph punishes coin made “com diversa materia ou sem o peso legal” more heavily: 2 to 8 years, against 1 to 4 for the perfect copy. Why does the imperfect imitation weigh more?
[10:30–12:00] And the French 20-franc coin struck in those years, the type minted from 1871 to 1898, with strikes in 1889 and 1890, is Augustin Dupré’s Génie: a winged genius writing the Constitution of 1793. France puts the Constitution in gold. So what is counterfeit coin, in law? Article 239 of the Penal Code of 1890 (text checked against the original publication of Decree 847 and the Leis Usuaes of 1903): to manufacture, without legitimate authority, coin “of identical matter, with the same form, weight and intrinsic value as the genuine one”. That is my translation. The legal authentication of the coin indexes matter, form, weight and intrinsic value. The effigy does not appear in the offence. Appearance does not authorise; the backing does. The woman on the metal represents the State; what authenticates the value is the matter the coin weighs. And an open question, which I hand to you as a problem, without a dogmatic answer. The sole paragraph of article 239 punishes more heavily, 2 to 8 years against 1 to 4 for the perfect copy, the coin made “with different matter or without the legal weight”. Why does the imperfect imitation weigh more? (Paper money follows other offences, arts. 240 and 241.) [If 10 minutes: keep 1.154 and art. 239 intact.]
