Back to the stairs: two regimes of the image
Seals: abstraction
Decree n. 4, 19 Nov 1889, fixes flag, arms and seals with non-figurative symbols. Abstraction authenticates the acts.
Coin and palace: a body
1906: the 1000 réis coin with the Effigy of the Republic (BR-1000R-1906), a white, European-featured profile.
VISUAL SEXUAL CONTRACT
The female figure authorises the order without being authorised by it. Brazilian women vote only from 1932; the married woman is relatively incapable under the Civil Code of 1916.
Ana Vanzin · Between Representation and Authentication
11
1000 réis, Effigy of the Republic, 1906–1912 series (specimen 1907). ICONOCRACIA corpus, BR-1000R-1906.
[12:00–13:30] The Republic itself produces a contrast. Decree n. 4, of 19 November 1889, fixes the flag, the arms and the seals with non-figurative symbols: abstraction authenticates the acts. Yet in the metal the citizen holds in the hand, and in the palace where government sits, lives the female figure. In 1906 the State issues the 1000 réis coin with the Effigy of the Republic, in our corpus as BR-1000R-1906: a white face, in profile, with European features, which makes the Republic visible as a white entity. The Visual Racial Contract as an internal operation. [If 10 minutes: cut the 1906 coin.] The Visual Sexual Contract names the paradox: the female figure authorises the order without being authorised by it. While the cast Marianne sits at the foot of the stairs here, and the bronze one in the Catete, Brazilian women stay outside the vote until 1932, and the married woman remains relatively incapable under the Civil Code of 1916. The allegory circulates as value; the woman, as exception.
11

How this argument could fail
1
State allegories that work the same with figures of another gender: indifference to the figure’s gender.
2
Appropriation of the allegory accompanied by effective legal inclusion of women.
3
Indicators of the hardening of images that vary independently of legal practice.
In a fragmentary corpus, archival silence counts as a mechanism not yet demonstrated, and only as that.
She represents authority without authenticating it. In that interval the legal order becomes visible.
[13:30–15:00] To close, the Visual Sexual Contract does not describe a law of history. It has to be able to fail. Three observations would refute it. First, state allegories working in the same way with figures of another gender: indifference to the gender of the figure. Second, appropriation of the allegory accompanied by effective legal inclusion of women. Third, indicators of the hardening of images varying independently of legal practices. And a word of method: in a fragmentary corpus, archival silence means a mechanism not demonstrated. It does not mean a mechanism refuted. So the question of the Marianne at the foot of the stairs remains. She represents authority, and she does not authenticate it. Between representation and authentication there is an interval, and it is in that interval that the legal order lets itself be seen.
